    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Geography</span> is the study of the Earth's surface, its physical features, human populations, and the interactions between people and their environment. The word comes from the Greek words <span class=\"learn-keyword\">geo</span> (Earth) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">graphein</span> (to write or describe).\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Definition</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Geography is defined as the science that studies the Earth's surface, the distribution of natural and human phenomena on it, and the relationships between people and their environment.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nature of Geography</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Geography is both a <strong>natural science</strong> (it studies physical features like landforms, climate, and soils) and a <strong>social science</strong> (it studies human activities, population, and settlements). This dual nature makes it a unique and broad discipline.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Scope of Geography</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Physical Geography:</strong> Landforms, climate, weather, rivers, soils, vegetation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Human Geography:</strong> Population, settlements, agriculture, transport, industry.</li>\n  <li><strong>Regional Geography:</strong> Detailed study of specific areas or regions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cartography:</strong> Map-making and interpretation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Environmental Geography:</strong> Human-environment interactions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Value of Geography</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Helps us understand the world around us.</li>\n  <li>Aids in planning and development (infrastructure, agriculture, housing).</li>\n  <li>Informs environmental management and conservation.</li>\n  <li>Useful in navigation and exploration.</li>\n  <li>Essential for understanding climate change and natural disasters.</li>\n  <li>Provides careers in surveying, town planning, meteorology, and cartography.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Branches of Geography</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Physical Geography</td><td>Natural features: landforms, climate, rivers, soils</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Human Geography</td><td>People: population, culture, economy, settlements</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Biogeography</td><td>Distribution of plants and animals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cartography</td><td>Map-making and map reading</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Climatology</td><td>Long-term atmospheric conditions</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Geography comes from Greek — geo (Earth) + graphein (to write). Know the five branches: physical, human, biogeography, cartography, climatology. Know at least five values of studying geography.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Geography is the science of the Earth's surface, natural features, and human activities. It has two main aspects — physical and human. Its value includes understanding the environment, planning, navigation, and career opportunities.\n</div>\n  ",
