    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Earth</span> is the third planet from the Sun and the only known planet that supports life. It orbits the Sun in the Solar System alongside seven other planets. Understanding the Earth's characteristics and how it compares to other planets is foundational to geography.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Solar System</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The Solar System consists of the Sun and all celestial bodies that orbit it. The eight planets in order from the Sun are: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Memory aid:</strong> \"My Very Energetic Mother Just Served Us Noodles\"</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Characteristics of the Earth</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Shape:</strong> The Earth is an <strong>oblate spheroid</strong> — slightly flattened at the poles and bulging at the equator.</li>\n  <li><strong>Size:</strong> Mean radius ≈ 6,371 km; circumference ≈ 40,075 km at the equator.</li>\n  <li><strong>Distance from Sun:</strong> About 150 million km (1 Astronomical Unit — AU).</li>\n  <li><strong>Atmosphere:</strong> Contains 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, and 1% other gases — supports life.</li>\n  <li><strong>Water:</strong> About 71% of Earth's surface is covered by water (oceans, lakes, rivers).</li>\n  <li><strong>Magnetic field:</strong> Protects life from harmful solar radiation.</li>\n  <li><strong>One moon (natural satellite).</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Average surface temperature:</strong> About 15°C — suitable for life.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Comparison with Other Planets</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Planet</th><th>Position</th><th>Key Feature</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mercury</td><td>1st</td><td>Smallest, no atmosphere, extreme temperatures</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Venus</td><td>2nd</td><td>Hottest planet, thick CO₂ atmosphere, no life</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Earth</td><td>3rd</td><td>Only planet with life, liquid water, oxygen atmosphere</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mars</td><td>4th</td><td>Red planet, thin CO₂ atmosphere, possible past water</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>5th</td><td>Largest planet, gas giant, 95 moons</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Saturn</td><td>6th</td><td>Gas giant, famous rings, least dense planet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uranus</td><td>7th</td><td>Ice giant, rotates on its side</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Neptune</td><td>8th</td><td>Farthest, strongest winds, ice giant</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Why Earth Supports Life</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Located in the <strong>habitable zone</strong> (not too hot, not too cold).</li>\n  <li>Has liquid water on its surface.</li>\n  <li>Has an oxygen-nitrogen atmosphere.</li>\n  <li>Has a protective magnetic field (magnetosphere).</li>\n  <li>Has moderate gravity to retain its atmosphere.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Earth's shape = oblate spheroid (not a perfect sphere). Order of planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune. Earth is the only planet with liquid water and life. Jupiter is the largest; Mercury is the smallest.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Solar System has 8 planets. Earth (3rd from Sun) is unique — oblate spheroid, 71% water surface, oxygen atmosphere, moderate temperature, one moon, and the only known planet supporting life. Jupiter is largest; Mercury is smallest.\n</div>\n  ",
