    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The Earth has two major movements: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">rotation</span> (spinning on its axis) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">revolution</span> (orbiting the Sun). These two movements are responsible for day and night, the seasons, and other important geographical phenomena.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Rotation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Rotation is the spinning of the Earth on its own axis from <strong>west to east</strong> (anticlockwise when viewed from the North Pole). One complete rotation takes approximately <strong>24 hours</strong> (one day).</p>\n<h4>Effects of Rotation:</h4>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Day and night:</strong> The part of Earth facing the Sun has day; the part facing away has night.</li>\n  <li><strong>Apparent movement of Sun and stars:</strong> The Sun appears to rise in the east and set in the west.</li>\n  <li><strong>Time zones:</strong> Different longitudes experience different times. The Earth is divided into 24 time zones (15° longitude each).</li>\n  <li><strong>Deflection of winds and ocean currents (Coriolis effect):</strong> To the right in the Northern Hemisphere; to the left in the Southern Hemisphere.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Revolution</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Revolution is the movement of the Earth around the Sun in an <strong>elliptical orbit</strong>. One complete revolution takes approximately <strong>365¼ days</strong> (one year). The extra quarter day is accounted for by adding a leap year every 4 years (366 days).</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Earth's axis is tilted at <strong>23.5°</strong> from the vertical to the plane of its orbit. This tilt is the main cause of seasons.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Seasons</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Season (Northern Hemisphere)</th><th>Approx. Dates</th><th>Sun position</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Summer</td><td>June–August</td><td>Sun over Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Autumn</td><td>Sept–Nov</td><td>Sun over Equator</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Winter</td><td>Dec–Feb</td><td>Sun over Tropic of Capricorn (23.5°S)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Spring</td><td>March–May</td><td>Sun over Equator</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Solstices and Equinoxes</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Summer Solstice (June 21):</strong> Sun directly overhead the Tropic of Cancer; longest day in Northern Hemisphere.</li>\n  <li><strong>Winter Solstice (December 22):</strong> Sun directly overhead the Tropic of Capricorn; shortest day in Northern Hemisphere.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vernal Equinox (March 21):</strong> Sun over Equator; day and night equal everywhere.</li>\n  <li><strong>Autumnal Equinox (September 23):</strong> Sun over Equator; day and night equal everywhere.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Rotation (24 hrs) = day/night + time zones + Coriolis effect. Revolution (365¼ days) = seasons + solstices + equinoxes. Earth's axis tilt = 23.5°. June 21 = summer solstice (N. Hemisphere). December 22 = winter solstice. March 21 and September 23 = equinoxes.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Rotation (24 hrs, W→E): day/night, time zones, Coriolis effect. Revolution (365¼ days, elliptical orbit): seasons. Earth's 23.5° tilt causes seasons. Solstices: June 21 (summer N. Hemi.) and December 22 (winter N. Hemi.). Equinoxes: March 21 and September 23.\n</div>\n  ",
