    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Latitudes</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">longitudes</span> are imaginary lines drawn on the Earth's surface to create a grid system that allows us to locate any point on Earth precisely. Together they form the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">geographical coordinate system</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Latitudes (Parallels)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Latitudes are imaginary horizontal lines drawn parallel to the Equator. They measure the angular distance north or south of the Equator.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Equator:</strong> 0° latitude — the longest parallel; divides Earth into Northern and Southern Hemispheres.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tropic of Cancer:</strong> 23.5°N — northernmost point Sun is directly overhead.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tropic of Capricorn:</strong> 23.5°S — southernmost point Sun is directly overhead.</li>\n  <li><strong>Arctic Circle:</strong> 66.5°N — boundary of the Arctic region.</li>\n  <li><strong>Antarctic Circle:</strong> 66.5°S — boundary of the Antarctic region.</li>\n  <li><strong>North Pole:</strong> 90°N; <strong>South Pole:</strong> 90°S.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">All lines of latitude are parallel to each other. They are circles (not semi-circles). They become smaller moving toward the poles.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Longitudes (Meridians)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Longitudes are imaginary vertical lines running from the North Pole to the South Pole. They measure the angular distance east or west of the Prime Meridian.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Prime Meridian (Greenwich Meridian):</strong> 0° longitude — passes through Greenwich, England.</li>\n  <li>Longitudes range from 0° to 180° East and 0° to 180° West.</li>\n  <li><strong>International Date Line:</strong> 180° longitude — crossing it changes the date by ±1 day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">All lines of longitude are equal in length (semi-circles). They converge at the poles.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Uses of Latitudes and Longitudes</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Precise location of any point on Earth.</li>\n  <li>Navigation (ships, aircraft, GPS).</li>\n  <li>Time calculation (1° longitude = 4 minutes time difference).</li>\n  <li>Determining climate zones based on latitude.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Time Calculation Using Longitude</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Earth rotates 360° in 24 hours = 15° per hour = 1° per 4 minutes. Moving east means adding time; moving west means subtracting time.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> If Lagos is at 3°E and Greenwich (0°) is 12:00 noon, Lagos time = 12:00 + (3 × 4 minutes) = 12:12 pm.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Latitudes = horizontal parallels (N/S of equator). Longitudes = vertical meridians (E/W of Prime Meridian). Equator = 0° lat. Prime Meridian = 0° long. 1° longitude = 4 minutes time difference. East = add time; West = subtract time.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Latitudes measure N/S from Equator (0°) to poles (90°). Key parallels: Equator (0°), Tropics (23.5°), Arctic/Antarctic Circles (66.5°). Longitudes measure E/W from Prime Meridian. 1° longitude = 4 minutes. GPS uses lat/long coordinates.\n</div>\n  ",
