    "topic": "The Earth's Structure",
    "topicCode": "SS1-GEO-05",
    "module": "Physical Geography",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The Earth is not a solid uniform mass — it has distinct <span class=\"learn-keyword\">layers</span> with different compositions, temperatures, and densities. Understanding the Earth's internal structure is important for explaining earthquakes, volcanic activity, and the formation of rocks and landforms.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Three Main Layers</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Depth</th><th>Composition</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Crust</td><td>0–70 km</td><td>Silica, aluminium, iron, magnesium</td><td>Solid</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mantle</td><td>70–2,900 km</td><td>Iron, magnesium silicates</td><td>Semi-solid (plastic)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Core</td><td>2,900–6,371 km</td><td>Iron and nickel</td><td>Outer core: liquid; Inner core: solid</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Crust</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The crust is the thin outermost layer of the Earth. There are two types:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Continental crust (SIAL):</strong> Thicker (up to 70 km); made of silica and aluminium; less dense. Forms continents.</li>\n  <li><strong>Oceanic crust (SIMA):</strong> Thinner (about 5–10 km); made of silica and magnesium; denser. Forms ocean floors.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Mantle</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The mantle is the thickest layer (about 2,830 km thick). It is composed of iron and magnesium silicate rocks. The upper part of the mantle is called the <strong>asthenosphere</strong> — a semi-plastic zone on which tectonic plates float and move.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The Core</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Outer core:</strong> Liquid iron and nickel; responsible for Earth's magnetic field.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inner core:</strong> Solid iron and nickel; temperatures reach about 5,000–6,000°C.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Importance of Earth's Structure</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The core produces Earth's magnetic field, protecting life.</li>\n  <li>The mantle's convection currents drive plate tectonics (causing earthquakes and volcanoes).</li>\n  <li>The crust provides the foundation for all life, soil, and human activities.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Three layers: Crust (thinnest, solid), Mantle (thickest, semi-solid), Core (hottest; outer = liquid, inner = solid). Continental crust = SIAL (silica + aluminium). Oceanic crust = SIMA (silica + magnesium). The liquid outer core creates Earth's magnetic field.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Earth has three layers: crust (solid, 0–70 km), mantle (semi-solid, 70–2,900 km), and core (2,900–6,371 km; outer liquid, inner solid). Continental crust = SIAL; oceanic crust = SIMA. Mantle convection drives plate movement.\n</div>\n  ",
