    "topic": "Rocks",
    "topicCode": "SS1-GEO-06",
    "module": "Physical Geography",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Rocks</span> are naturally occurring solid mixtures of minerals that make up the Earth's crust. There are three main types of rocks based on how they are formed: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">igneous</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">sedimentary</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">metamorphic</span>. These types are continually being transformed into each other through the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">rock cycle</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Igneous Rocks</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Formed by the cooling and solidification of molten rock (magma or lava).</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Intrusive (plutonic):</strong> Magma cools slowly underground → large crystals. Example: <strong>Granite</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Extrusive (volcanic):</strong> Lava cools quickly on surface → small or no crystals. Example: <strong>Basalt</strong>, obsidian.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Characteristics:</strong> Hard, resistant to erosion; no fossils; crystalline.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Sedimentary Rocks</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Formed by the compaction and cementation of sediments (fragments of other rocks, minerals, or organic matter) deposited by water, wind, or ice.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Clastic:</strong> Formed from rock fragments. Examples: Sandstone, shale, conglomerate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chemical:</strong> Formed from minerals precipitated from solution. Example: Limestone (some types), rock salt.</li>\n  <li><strong>Organic:</strong> Formed from remains of organisms. Example: Coal, coral limestone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Characteristics:</strong> Layered (stratified); may contain fossils; found near rivers, seas, deserts.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Metamorphic Rocks</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Formed when existing rocks (igneous or sedimentary) are transformed by heat and pressure without melting.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Limestone → <strong>Marble</strong></li>\n  <li>Shale → <strong>Slate</strong></li>\n  <li>Sandstone → <strong>Quartzite</strong></li>\n  <li>Granite → <strong>Gneiss</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Characteristics:</strong> Very hard; banded or foliated; fossils destroyed by heat.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of Rocks</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Building materials: granite, marble, sandstone.</li>\n  <li>Soil formation: weathered rocks form soil.</li>\n  <li>Mineral resources: gold, iron ore, coal found in rocks.</li>\n  <li>Tourism: scenic landforms made of rocks.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Three rock types: Igneous (cooling magma/lava), Sedimentary (compacted sediments, has fossils, layered), Metamorphic (heat + pressure transforms existing rocks). Key examples: Granite (igneous), Sandstone (sedimentary), Marble (metamorphic). Marble comes from limestone.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Igneous (magma/lava solidifies), Sedimentary (sediments compacted; layered; has fossils), Metamorphic (heat + pressure; very hard; no fossils). Rock cycle: rocks continuously transform between types. Examples: Granite, Sandstone, Marble.\n</div>\n  ",
