    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Mountains</span> are large landforms that rise steeply above the surrounding landscape. They are formed by different geological processes and have significant influences on climate, settlement, and economic activities.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Mountains</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Formation</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fold Mountains</td><td>Compressional forces fold rock layers into ridges and valleys</td><td>Himalayas (Asia), Alps (Europe), Rockies (N. America)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Block Mountains (Horsts)</td><td>Tensional forces cause blocks of land to be uplifted between faults</td><td>Black Forest (Germany), Vosges (France), Ruwenzori (Africa)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Volcanic Mountains</td><td>Accumulation of lava, ash, and other materials from volcanic eruptions</td><td>Mt. Kilimanjaro (Tanzania), Mt. Etna (Italy), Mt. Cameroon (West Africa)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Residual (Relict) Mountains</td><td>Resistant rocks remain after surrounding softer rocks are eroded</td><td>Adamawa Highlands (Nigeria/Cameroon), Aso Rock (Nigeria)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Importance of Mountains</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Climate influence:</strong> Mountains affect rainfall (orographic rainfall on windward slopes; rain shadow on leeward side).</li>\n  <li><strong>Water source:</strong> Mountains are sources of major rivers (Himalayas → Ganges, Indus).</li>\n  <li><strong>Tourism:</strong> Scenic mountain landscapes attract tourists (skiing, hiking).</li>\n  <li><strong>Mineral resources:</strong> Mountains contain rich mineral deposits.</li>\n  <li><strong>Defence:</strong> Mountains serve as natural barriers and defensive positions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vegetation zones:</strong> Mountains have distinct vegetation zones from base to summit.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Four mountain types: Fold (compression → folded rock), Block/Horst (faulting → uplifted block), Volcanic (lava accumulation), Residual (erosion leaves hard rock). Key examples: Himalayas (fold), Kilimanjaro (volcanic), Ruwenzori (block), Aso Rock (residual). Mt. Cameroon is West Africa's highest volcanic mountain.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Mountains: fold (compression), block (faulting), volcanic (lava), residual (erosion). Importance: climate/rainfall, river sources, tourism, minerals, defence, vegetation zones. Mt. Cameroon = West Africa's highest; Kilimanjaro = Africa's highest.\n</div>\n  ",
