    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Climate</span> is the average weather conditions of a place over a long period, typically <span class=\"learn-keyword\">30 years or more</span>. While weather changes daily, climate describes the typical atmospheric conditions of a region. Climate influences vegetation, agriculture, settlement, and all aspects of human life.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Difference from Weather</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Climate</strong> = long-term average of weather (temperature, rainfall, etc.) over 30+ years.<br><strong>Weather</strong> = short-term conditions at a specific time.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">\"Climate is what you expect; weather is what you get.\"</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Elements of Climate</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The elements of climate are the same as weather — temperature, rainfall, humidity, wind, cloud cover, sunshine, and pressure — but averaged over many years.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Factors Influencing Climate</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on Climate</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Latitude</td><td>Areas near the Equator are hotter; temperature decreases toward poles</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Altitude</td><td>Temperature decreases with altitude (~6.5°C per 1,000 m)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Distance from the sea (continentality)</td><td>Coastal areas are moderate; inland areas have extreme temperatures</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ocean currents</td><td>Warm currents raise temperature; cold currents lower temperature</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prevailing winds</td><td>Onshore winds bring moisture; offshore winds are dry</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Relief (mountains)</td><td>Windward slopes receive rain; leeward slopes are dry (rain shadow)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Natural vegetation</td><td>Forests moderate climate; deforestation causes extreme conditions</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Major Climate Zones</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Equatorial:</strong> Hot and wet all year; near 0°N/S. Example: Congo Basin.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tropical:</strong> Hot with distinct wet and dry seasons. Example: most of Nigeria.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sahel/Semi-arid:</strong> Hot and dry with very little rainfall. Example: northern Nigeria.</li>\n  <li><strong>Desert:</strong> Very hot and very dry. Example: Sahara Desert.</li>\n  <li><strong>Temperate:</strong> Moderate temperatures; found in mid-latitudes. Example: Europe.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Climate = 30-year average; Weather = short-term. Seven factors affecting climate: latitude, altitude, distance from sea, ocean currents, prevailing winds, relief, vegetation. Latitude is the most important factor — it determines how directly the Sun's rays hit the Earth.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Climate = long-term (30 years) average weather. Same elements as weather. Factors: latitude (most important), altitude, distance from sea, ocean currents, prevailing winds, relief, vegetation. Climate zones: equatorial, tropical, Sahel, desert, temperate.\n</div>\n  ",
