    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Nigeria's <span class=\"learn-keyword\">physical setting</span> encompasses its relief (landforms), drainage (rivers and lakes), and vegetation zones. These physical features have shaped human settlement patterns, agricultural activities, and economic development across the country.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Relief of Nigeria</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Nigeria's relief can be divided into the following regions:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Coastal Lowlands (South):</strong> Low-lying, swampy, mangrove-covered; includes Niger Delta.</li>\n  <li><strong>Western Lowlands:</strong> Low plateau with scattered hills; forested in places.</li>\n  <li><strong>Eastern Highlands:</strong> Includes Obudu Plateau, Shebshi Mountains; highest point in Nigeria — Chappal Waddi (2,419 m) in Taraba State.</li>\n  <li><strong>Central Plateau (Jos Plateau):</strong> Elevation 1,200–1,300 m; cooler climate; tin and columbite mining.</li>\n  <li><strong>Northern Plains:</strong> Flat, low-lying; Sudan Savanna and Sahel vegetation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Niger-Benue Trough:</strong> Broad river valleys of the Niger and Benue rivers.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Drainage of Nigeria</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Nigeria's major rivers include:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Niger River:</strong> Enters from Guinea/Mali; flows SE through Nigeria before emptying into the Gulf of Guinea via the Niger Delta.</li>\n  <li><strong>Benue River:</strong> Rises in Cameroon; the major tributary of the Niger; joins the Niger at Lokoja.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cross River:</strong> Drains the southeastern states into the Bight of Biafra.</li>\n  <li><strong>Kaduna, Sokoto, Gongola Rivers:</strong> Other significant rivers in northern and central Nigeria.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lake Chad:</strong> A shrinking inland lake in northeast Nigeria, shared with Niger, Chad, and Cameroon.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Vegetation Zones of Nigeria</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Location</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mangrove Forest</td><td>Coastal south</td><td>Salt-tolerant trees; Niger Delta swamps</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tropical Rainforest</td><td>Southwest and Southeast</td><td>Dense, multilayered evergreen forest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Guinea Savanna</td><td>Central Nigeria</td><td>Tall grass with trees; most extensive zone</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sudan Savanna</td><td>North-central</td><td>Shorter grass, more open woodland</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sahel Savanna</td><td>Far north</td><td>Sparse grass and drought-resistant shrubs; near desert</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Nigeria's highest point = Chappal Waddi (2,419 m, Taraba State). Niger and Benue join at Lokoja. Vegetation zones from south to north: Mangrove → Rainforest → Guinea Savanna → Sudan Savanna → Sahel Savanna. Jos Plateau = tin and columbite mining, cooler climate.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Relief: coastal lowlands, central Jos Plateau, eastern highlands (Chappal Waddi 2,419 m), northern plains. Major rivers: Niger, Benue (join at Lokoja), Cross River. Vegetation S→N: Mangrove → Rainforest → Guinea Savanna → Sudan Savanna → Sahel.\n</div>\n  ",
