    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Nigeria is Africa's most populous country with over <span class=\"learn-keyword\">200 million people</span>. Understanding Nigeria's population — its size, distribution, density, and growth — is essential for planning education, healthcare, infrastructure, and economic development.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Population Size and Growth</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Nigeria's population is approximately 216 million (2023 estimate). At independence in 1960 it was about 45 million. The population has grown rapidly due to high birth rates and declining death rates.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Natural growth</strong> = Birth rate − Death rate.</li>\n  <li>Nigeria's birth rate ≈ 37 per 1,000; death rate ≈ 11 per 1,000.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Population Distribution and Density</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Population is <strong>unevenly distributed</strong>. Dense areas: Lagos, Kano, Onitsha. Sparse areas: Niger State, Taraba, Borno.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Population density = Total population ÷ Total area. Nigeria's average ≈ 218 persons/km².</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Factors Influencing Population Distribution</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Relief and Terrain</td><td>Flat fertile land attracts settlement; swamps and mountains repel</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Climate and Rainfall</td><td>Adequate rainfall attracts farming populations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Economic Activities</td><td>Industrial and commercial areas attract workers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Security</td><td>Insecure areas are depopulated; safe areas attract migrants</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Population density = total population ÷ area. Nigeria ≈ 200+ million (Africa's most populous). Dense: Lagos, Kano. Sparse: Niger State, Taraba. Natural increase = Birth rate − Death rate.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigeria: 200+ million, Africa's most. Uneven distribution. Density = pop ÷ area. Factors: terrain, climate, economy, security.\n</div>\n  ",
