    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Measuring distances on maps and converting them to actual ground distances using the map <span class=\"learn-keyword\">scale</span> is a fundamental cartographic skill.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Straight-line Distance</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Use a ruler to measure map distance in cm, then: <strong>Actual distance = Map distance × Scale denominator</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> 3 cm on a 1:100,000 map = 3 × 100,000 = 300,000 cm = 3 km.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Winding/Curved Distance</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>String method:</strong> Lay thread along the route; measure thread length; apply scale.</li>\n  <li><strong>Paperedge method:</strong> Mark bends on paper edge; straighten and measure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dividers method:</strong> Walk fixed intervals along route; count steps × interval.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key Formulas</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Actual = Map distance × Denominator</li>\n  <li>Map distance = Actual ÷ Denominator</li>\n  <li>1 km = 1,000 m = 100,000 cm</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always convert to same units before calculating. 1 km = 100,000 cm. Curved routes: string or paperedge. Actual = map × denominator; Map = actual ÷ denominator.\n</div>\n  ",
