    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Map enlargement</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">reduction</span> change the scale of a map while maintaining proportional accuracy.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Concepts</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Enlargement:</strong> Smaller RF denominator → bigger map, more detail.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reduction:</strong> Larger RF denominator → smaller map, less detail.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Scale Factor</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Linear Scale Factor (SF) = Old denominator ÷ New denominator.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> 1:50,000 → 1:25,000: SF = 50,000 ÷ 25,000 = 2 (enlarged ×2).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Effect on Area</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Area changes by linear SF². If SF=2 (linear): area × 4. If SF=3: area × 9.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Grid Method</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Draw a proportionally larger/smaller grid on the new map and transfer features square by square.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Linear SF = old ÷ new denominator. Area SF = linear SF². Enlargement: smaller denominator. Reduction: larger denominator.\n</div>\n  ",
