    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Transportation</span> is the movement of people, goods, and services from one place to another. Nigeria has four main modes: road, rail, water, and air.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Road Transport</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The most common mode in Nigeria. Over 195,000 km of roads. Flexible, door-to-door. Problems: poor conditions, congestion, accidents.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Rail Transport</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Cheap for bulk goods. Modernised lines: Lagos-Ibadan, Abuja-Kaduna. Problems: limited network, high construction cost.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Water Transport</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Cheapest for bulk goods. Rivers: Niger, Benue, Cross River. Ports: Lagos (Apapa), Port Harcourt, Warri. Problems: slow, seasonal, limited to waterways.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Air Transport</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Fastest, most expensive. International airports: Lagos (Murtala Muhammed), Abuja (Nnamdi Azikiwe), Port Harcourt, Kano. Good for perishables and high-value goods.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Road = most common. Water = cheapest (bulk). Air = fastest + most expensive. Major ports: Apapa (Lagos), Port Harcourt, Warri. Lagos airport = Murtala Muhammed; Abuja = Nnamdi Azikiwe.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Road (flexible, widespread), Rail (bulk, cheap), Water (cheapest, rivers/coasts), Air (fastest, expensive). Transport promotes trade and development.\n</div>\n  ",
